# SRG-APP-000080-AU-000010  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must be configured to protect the data sent from
# hosts and devices from being altered in a way that may prevent the
# attribution of an action to an individual (or process acting on behalf of
# an individual).
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000080-AU-000010
group:           V-206448
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000166
control_rev5:    AU-10

contribution:    Splunk can compute and store rawdata-slice hashes for every index
                 (enableDataIntegrityControl), so later alteration of indexed records is
                 detectable.

governs:         indexes.conf - The indexes data is stored in, how large they may grow and how long data
                 is kept.

check:           indexes.conf [<index name>] enableDataIntegrityControl in 1|true

external:        Filesystem permissions on the index rawdata and hash files - the operating
                 system, not Splunk, prevents direct tampering with bucket contents on
                 disk.
external:        Membership of the delete-capable role, and therefore who can actually
                 remove indexed records, is account provisioning and directory
                 administration, not a Splunk setting.

citation:        INVENTION. Removed authorize.conf [role_<roleName>] delete_by_keyword
                 absent: it cannot be satisfied. authorize.conf.spec gives a role
                 capability as "<capability> = <enabled>" and notes "'enabled' is the only
                 accepted value here, as capabilities are disabled by default", so a role
                 stanza cannot express a denial; and the product ships [role_can_delete]
                 with delete_by_keyword = enabled in system/default/authorize.conf, which
                 assess.sh reads through btool's merged view. The condition therefore fails
                 every stock installation. Restricting delete is real, but it is enforced
                 by not placing anyone in that role, which authorize.conf does not record,
                 so it moves to external_dependencies. The retained condition is the one
                 the benchmark authority actually specifies.
finding:         enableDataIntegrityControl is not set to 1 or true for the indexes in
                 scope, so Splunk does not compute or store the rawdata hashes needed to
                 detect later alteration of the records it received.
